# network-manager is only needed for "Direct mode" (the ad-hoc WiFi hotspot
# fallback, nearshare/core/hotspot.py's nmcli calls); bluez is only needed
# for the BLE trigger advertisement/scan (nearshare/core/ble.py) that lets
# a nearby Android phone notice this machine proactively. Both features
# degrade gracefully (mDNS-only, no Direct mode) when their backend is
# missing, so neither is a hard Depends.
# The Nautilus right-click "Send with NearShare" top-level menu item
# (debian/extra/nearshare_menu.py, installed system-wide at
# /usr/share/nautilus-python/extensions/) needs python3-nautilus to
# load. It's a Recommends (not a Depends) so apt installs it by default
# on a normal `apt install`/`apt install ./nearshare_*.deb` -- but the
# package still installs and everything else still works if it's
# declined or later removed; Nautilus just silently skips loading the
# extension file, same as it does with any nautilus-python extension
# when the interpreter/bindings aren't present.
Recommends: network-manager, bluez, python3-nautilus
Description: Quick Share (Nearby Share) for the GNOME desktop
 NearShare is a GTK4/libadwaita implementation of Google's Quick Share
 (formerly Nearby Share) wire protocol for Linux. It speaks the same mDNS
 discovery, BLE trigger advertising, UKEY2 key exchange, and encrypted
 transfer framing as the real thing, so it interoperates directly with
 Android Quick Share, Windows Quick Share, macOS NearDrop, and other
 NearShare instances -- no cables, no cloud account, and no companion
 app on the phone.
 .
 Every transfer is end-to-end encrypted (AES-256-CBC + HMAC-SHA256, keys
 from an ECDH exchange over P-256) and confirmed with a 4-digit PIN shown
 on both screens; NearShare has no Google-account trust, so every
 transfer needs a manual PIN confirmation.
 .
 Includes a GTK4 GUI, a `nearshare` CLI for scripting and keyboard
 shortcuts, and a Nautilus right-click "Send with NearShare" integration.
